  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nExpect a crisp morning with lows mainly in the mid 40s north to\nthe mid 50s elsewhere. The one exception will be southern New\nHampshire and the far southern tip of Maine where we may only \ndip into the lower 60s. Valley fog will be possible north as\nlight winds and clear skies promote efficient radiational\ncooling.\n\nA modest upper-level ridge axis will move slowly towards the \nregion today with strands of vorticity forecast to ripple \nthrough northwest flow aloft. At the surface, a stationary front\nremains in place, extending from around Lake Ontario to Long \nIsland, NY. A low is forecast to ride east southeast along this\nboundary, which will lead to some rain across southern New \nHampshire and portions of western Maine. The northern extent of \nthe rain remains a bit uncertain as moisture will be fighting \nagainst dry high pressure to the north. Some of the latest short\nterm guidance has trended a touch later with the precipitation \nonset and there has been a slight nudge south the past few runs \nof the HRRR. These trends could suggest high pressure winning \nout versus more widespread precipitation over the forecast area.\n\nAll of that said, confidence remains high that we should see \nsome steady rainfall over much of southern New Hampshire and \nportions of the western Maine, most likely the southwest. 24-hour\nNBM QPF probabilities suggest that the highest totals will be \nmaximized over southwest New Hampshire with amounts most likely \nin the half inch to one inch range. Totals will diminish rather\nquickly moving northeast.\n\nWe should see plenty of cloud cover today which will keep\ntemperatures on the cooler side. Afternoon highs are forecast to\nrange from the upper 60s to mid 70s. The coolest temperatures\nwill likely be across southern New Hampshire and southwest Maine\nwhere we will see the most rainy/cloudy conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWe find ourselves between shortwaves on Wednesday, which should lead \nto rain-free conditions during the day. Likely will still have to \ncontend with mostly cloudy skies and onshore flow, which suggests \nanother day of cooler highs, primarily in the 60s-near 70, but a bit \nof a muggy feel persisting, which will also lead to another mild \nnight of lows in the mid 50s-low 60s.\n\nThe second wave moves in for Thursday. Rain chances start as early \nas Wednesday night, but higher PoPs, and the chance for heavier \nrain, will likely be on Thursday when the best upper-level support \nand forcing arrives locally. Timing of precipitation remains to be \nironed out, with outcomes ranging from a soaking rain all day, to a \nfew rounds of more convective precipitation with some dry periods. \nStorm motions on the slower side, combined with a rather soupy \nairmass, suggests locally heavy rain may be the primary threat. Dew \npoints reach the 60s-near 70, and PWATs approaching 1.7-1.8\" in our \nsouthern zones. Around 30 kts of deep-layer shear suggest storms \ncould organize a bit, but guidance varies significantly at this time \non if instability will be able to build or not.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThursday's wave closes off and deepens over Nova Scotia Friday into \nearly weekend, and is slow to depart. Drying northerly flow out of \nCanada should flush out with humidity with an overall cooler \nairmass. This will lead to a taste of early fall especially at \nnight, but winds out of the north should also serve to downslope/act \nas an offshore wind, which will keep afternoons mild with 70s in the \nlower elevations.\n\nWith us still on the edge of the trough and cold core aloft, could \nsee some diurnal clouds and showers each afternoon, mostly focused \nin the mountains/interior, but not expecting anything high-impact or \nwidespread. Finally, it will be breezy near the coast with a rather \ntight pressure gradient setting up.",
